         \[U_j=\tfrac12 D_j+c\,\eta_j,\qquad V_j=\tfrac12 D_j-c\,\eta_j.\]
         <p>Each of \(U_j\) and \(V_j\) is a linear combination of the normal variables \(D_j\) and \(\eta_j\), which are independent (shown in the next paragraph). Assuming A1(a), \(\Cov(D_j,\eta_j)=0\), because independent integrable variables have \(\E[D_j\eta_j]=\E D_j\,\E\eta_j=0\). The cross term in \(\Var(U_j)=\tfrac14\Var(D_j)+c^2\Var(\eta_j)+c\,\Cov(D_j,\eta_j)\) therefore vanishes, and \(\Var(U_j)=\tfrac14\cdot 2^{-n}+c^2=\tfrac14\cdot 2^{-n}+2^{-(n+2)}=2^{-(n+1)}\). The variance of \(V_j\) is the same. Each is therefore normal with that variance, by A1(b).</p>
         <p>Independence. The variables \(D_1,\dots,D_{2^n}\) are functions of the coefficients \(\xi_0\) and \(\xi_{m,j}\) with \(m\le n\), and the \(\eta_j=\xi_{n+1,j}\) are functions of disjoint coefficients. By H1 and A1(e), the two groups are independent, and by the induction hypothesis the \(D_j\) are mutually independent. So, by A1(e) again, the \(2^{n+1}\) variables \(D_j,\eta_j\) are mutually independent. The pair \((U_j,V_j)\) is a function of \((D_j,\eta_j)\), and the pairs for different \(j\) are functions of disjoint blocks of this family. By A1(e), the pairs are mutually independent. Within a pair, \(U_j\) and \(V_j\) are jointly normal by A1(b), since both are combinations of the one family \(D_j,\eta_j\). Their covariance is \(\tfrac14\Var(D_j)-c^2\Var(\eta_j)=\tfrac14\cdot 2^{-n}-2^{-(n+2)}=0\); the cross terms cancel, because \(\tfrac12\cdot(-c)+c\cdot\tfrac12=0\) multiplies \(\Cov(D_j,\eta_j)\). By A1(c) they are independent. So, by A1(e) applied to the blocks \(\{U_j,V_j\}\), each internally independent, the \(2^{n+1}\) increments \(U_j,V_j\) are mutually independent, each \(\Normal(0,2^{-(n+1)})\). This completes the step.</p>
