-      <p class="why-it-matters">The sums of squared increments of Brownian motion do not vanish under refinement, unlike those of a smooth function. Each squared increment of \(B\) has mean equal to the length of its interval, so the sum of squared increments over a partition (defined in §1) has mean equal to the elapsed time, however fine the partition. For the smooth function \(s \mapsto s^2\) the same sums shrink to zero. This lesson proves both statements with explicit constants. The limit holds in mean square, that is, the expected squared error tends to zero under Assumptions 3.1 and 3.2 (stated below, in §1). Lab 1 simulates Brownian increments on partitions into \(2^k\) equal steps and prints the sample means with standard errors.</p>
